[TEXT_CALCDESC]
Accepted input formats for metadata files are the ones listed by \b
--list-toc-formats. Accepted input formats for audio files are the ones listed
by \b --list-audio-formats. An "audio format" is a combination of a codec and a
container file format. Which input formats are supported depends entirely on the
underlying instance of libarcsdec that is used.

If a metadata file is passed, there may or may not be passed audio files. The
audio input will always be considered to form an album in this case.

If a metadata file and no audio files are specified, the name of the audio files
must be present in the metadata file.

If a metadata file is passed along with a single audio file, the audiofile is
supposed to contain all tracks of the album. It overrides any audio filename
specified in the metadata file.

If a metadata file is passed along with multiple audiofiles, the total number of
audiofiles passed must match the number of tracks specified in the metadata
file. Otherwise the input will not be processed. In other words: either override
all filenames with a single audio file containing all tracks, or specify exactly
one distinct audio file for each track. Audio files are assigned to album tracks
in the order they are passed.

If audio files are passed without a metadata file, the input is by default not
treated as an album but each audio file is processed as an intermediate track.
The audio files are processed in the order their names are passed; beware that
when using wildcards, the order will be determined by your command-line
processor. Since the first and last tracks require special algorithmic
treatment, use \b --first or \b --last to declare the first or last audio file
passed as the corresponding album track. Options \b --first and \b --last can be
combined or used independently to treat incomplete albums. To declare the entire
input as an album, use \b --album, which implies \b --first and \b --last. Note
that when declaring \b --album, \b --first, or \b --last, the order of audio
files is significant.
[TEXT_CALCDESC]

